{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "heat_1d_varcoeff_dirichlet",
    "description": "MMS driver: 1-D VARIABLE-COEFFICIENT heat equation u_t = div(k(x) grad u) + S(x,t) on the unit interval [0, 1] with inhomogeneous DIRICHLET (fixed-value) boundaries, discretized by the conservative finite-volume rule grids/cartesian_uniform_1d/rules/varcoeff_laplacian_dirichlet_bc. The consumer writes the operator as the conservative compound D(k*D(u,x),x) (coefficient on the LEFT), which that rule matches at priority 10 and lowers to the arithmetic-mean flux-difference stencil with mirror-ghost fixed-value faces. The coefficient k(x) = 1 + 0.5 cos(2 pi x) is a consumer-supplied observed of shape [x] (positive on [0,1], range [0.5,1.5]); it is read by the rule body at the interior faces via kf[i+1/2]=(k[i]+k[i+1])/2, and the boundary faces use the wall conductivities k[1], k[N]. Manufactured solution u(x,t) = exp(-4 pi^2 t) sin^2(pi x) + gL with gL = gR = 0.5: the decaying sin^2(pi x) = 0.5 - 0.5 cos(2 pi x) vanishes AND has zero gradient at both walls, so the wall values are the constant offset 0.5 at both x=0 and x=1 (constant in time), matched by the rule's free names gL = gR = 0.5. Choosing a mode with ZERO wall gradient is deliberate: the cell-centered mirror-ghost boundary flux 2 k[1] (u[1]-gL)/dx uses the wall conductivity k[1], whose O(dx) offset from k(x0) would multiply a nonzero wall gradient into a variable-coefficient-specific O(dx) boundary term and cause fine-grid error cancellation (a non-asymptotic super-2 order); with u_x=0 at the walls that cross term vanishes, leaving the SAME benign boundary error as the constant-coefficient central_D2_dirichlet_bc, so both L2 and Linf converge cleanly at order 2. The constant offset 0.5 is discretely exact (interior flux difference and both mirror-ghost face residuals vanish identically), so the discretization error comes solely from the sin^2 mode. Because sin^2(pi x) is NOT an eigenfunction of the variable-coefficient operator, a forcing term S(x,t) = u_t - div(k grad u) is added so u is exact for the continuous PDE. Derivation: with k' = -pi sin(2 pi x), div(k grad(exp(-4 pi^2 t) sin^2 pi x)) = exp(-4 pi^2 t)[2 pi^2 cos(2 pi x) + pi^2 cos(4 pi x)] and u_t = -4 pi^2 exp(-4 pi^2 t) sin^2(pi x) = exp(-4 pi^2 t)[-2 pi^2 + 2 pi^2 cos(2 pi x)]; choosing lambda = 4 pi^2 cancels the cos(2 pi x) terms, leaving the closed form S(x,t) = exp(-4 pi^2 t)(-2 pi^2 - pi^2 cos(4 pi x)), spelled below as a time factor exp(-4 pi^2 t) times an aggregate over cell centers. The grid's consumer-supplied geometry contract (grids/cartesian_uniform_1d/grid.esm) is fulfilled for [0,1]: x0 = 0 (parameter) and dx = 1/N (an observed dividing by the metaparameter name N, substituted at load per esm-spec 9.7.6). gL/gR appear only in the runtime rule body; initial condition, coefficient, forcing, and test references all spell the cell-center coordinate x_i = (i - 1/2)/N inline with load-foldable scalars (AGENTS.md build-time scope caveat), with the wall constant 0.5 matching the gL/gR parameter values and the ic written in the identity form sin^2(pi x)+0.5 = 1 - 0.5 cos(2 pi x). The SAME rule also serves the nonlinear case (a consumer declaring k as an observed k=f(u)); this driver exercises the variable-coefficient dual.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_1d",
      "equation:heat",
      "mms"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Roache, P. J. (2002). Code verification by the method of manufactured solutions. J. Fluids Eng. 124(1), 4-10.",
        "doi": "10.1115/1.1436090"
      },
      {
        "citation": "LeVeque, R. J. (2007). Finite Difference Methods for Ordinary and Partial Differential Equations. SIAM. Section 2.15 (variable-coefficient conservative form) and Section 2.12 (cell-centered Dirichlet)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "Heat": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../grids/cartesian_uniform_1d/rules/varcoeff_laplacian_dirichlet_bc.esm"
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "u": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "x"
          ],
          "description": "Temperature field over the x index set."
        },
        "gL": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "K",
          "default": 0.5,
          "description": "Prescribed field value at the left wall x=x0 (the rule's Dirichlet free name), equal to the manufactured wall value u(0,t) = 0.5 (the sin^2 mode vanishes there)."
        },
        "gR": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "K",
          "default": 0.5,
          "description": "Prescribed field value at the right wall x=x0+N*dx (the rule's Dirichlet free name), equal to the manufactured wall value u(1,t) = 0.5 (the sin^2 mode vanishes there)."
        },
        "x0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0,
          "description": "Left domain edge a = 0 (unit interval): the grid's x0 free name (consumer-supplied geometry contract, grids/cartesian_uniform_1d/grid.esm)."
        },
        "dx": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "description": "Grid spacing (b - a)/N = 1/N: the grid's dx free name, resolved inside the rewrite rule's expanded body at evaluation. N is the document-scoped metaparameter, substituted as an integer literal at load (esm-spec 9.7.6), so loader-API rebinding for the convergence sweep keeps dx consistent."
        },
        "k": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s",
          "shape": [
            "x"
          ],
          "description": "Variable conductivity k(x) = 1 + 0.5 cos(2 pi x) over the x index set, evaluated at cell centers x_gi = (gi - 1/2)/N (spelled inline from literals and the metaparameter name N). Positive on [0,1] (range [0.5,1.5]). Bound by the rule's match as the coefficient k (the left factor of k*D(u,x)) and read by the expanded stencil at the faces. The aggregate loops over `gi`, NOT the stencil's interior index `i`: an array coefficient authored as an aggregate observed is inlined into the stencil's [2,N-1] aggregate at evaluation, and a shared loop-index name would be captured (see the rule description)."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "lhs": {
            "op": "ic",
            "args": [
              "u"
            ]
          },
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "output_idx": [
